    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>In garden design, a fence is no longer just a boundary. It is an architectural element that shapes the character of the whole plot. Vertical boards and panels have long been the default, but the horizontal slat fence has become a favourite for homeowners who want a contemporary look, with long, clean lines that give a modern home real kerb appeal.</p>
<p>Choosing a horizontal layout is about more than looks, though. It asks more of the materials, the structure and the installer than a standard panel fence. This guide sets out the honest pros and cons, the materials worth considering and the kinds of homes and gardens the style suits best.</p>

<h2 id="pros">1. The pros of a horizontal slat fence</h2>

<h3>A clean, modern look</h3>
<p>The main reason people choose horizontal slats is the aesthetic. Traditional vertical fences tend to read as rustic or suburban, which can clash with contemporary architecture. Horizontal lines echo the profiles of modern homes and emphasise width and flow.</p>
<p>Horizontal lines also carry the eye across the garden rather than up and down, which gives a sense of continuity. For mid-century, industrial or minimalist houses, a slatted fence works as an extension of the building's own geometry and a calm backdrop that lets planting and architectural details stand out.</p>

<h3>It makes a garden feel wider</h3>
<p>One of the most useful functional benefits is the way horizontal slats change how big a space feels. Much like horizontal stripes, long slats draw the eye sideways, so a small urban garden or a narrow side return feels less boxed in. The boundary seems to recede, and a modest seating area feels more open. Garden designers use this effect deliberately in tight plots, and we cover it in more detail in our guide to <a href="/blog/horizontal-slat-fence-small-yard-ideas">horizontal slat fence ideas for small yards</a>.</p>

<h3>Privacy you can fine-tune</h3>
<p>Because horizontal fences are usually built on site rather than from ready-made panels, you control the gap between slats. Fix the boards tight together for a near-solid screen, or leave small, even gaps, often somewhere around 5–25 mm (¼–1 in), to let air and dappled light through. That flexibility lets you balance a private retreat against a fence that does not feel like a fortress.</p>
<p>A taller fence also helps screen views from neighbouring upstairs windows, but check the height limits before you plan one. In England, a fence or wall over 2 m (6 ft 7 in) high, or over 1 m (3 ft 3 in) next to a highway used by vehicles, usually needs planning permission. Listed buildings, conservation areas and homes with planning conditions may have extra restrictions. In the US, limits vary by city, county and HOA. Always check with your local planning authority before you build.</p>

<h3>Easy to customise</h3>
<p>A horizontal fence is essentially a series of layers, so it is easy to personalise. You can alternate wide and narrow boards for a rhythmic, textured look along a long run, mix timber species, or pair natural timber slats with black metal posts for an industrial feel. Hidden fixings give a seamless, "floating" finish, and the format works equally well with a natural oiled finish, a painted colour or reclaimed boards.</p>

<h3>Durable when built properly</h3>
<p>A well-built horizontal fence can be very sturdy. The slats are usually screwed directly to the face of the posts rather than hung as panels, and with good materials such as dense hardwood, cedar, composite or metal it copes well with the weather. How long it lasts depends on the material, the installation, your climate and the upkeep, so check any manufacturer's or supplier's warranty for its stated term.</p>

<h2 id="cons">2. The cons of a horizontal slat fence</h2>

<h3>Installation needs real precision</h3>
<p>Installation is the biggest drawback. On a vertical fence, a slight error in level often goes unnoticed. With horizontal slats, every board must be level, and a tiny tilt at one end becomes obvious by the time the run reaches the other side of the garden. Gaps need to stay consistent too, which usually means spacers and careful checking with a level as you go. It is a job for an experienced DIYer or a professional installer.</p>

<h3>More posts and a stronger frame</h3>
<p>Long horizontal boards can bow or sag under their own weight between supports. To prevent this, posts are usually set closer than for vertical fencing — often around 1.2–1.8 m (4–6 ft) apart, depending on board thickness and the supplier's guidance. That means more holes to dig, more posts and more concrete or post fixings, which adds to both the materials and the labour.</p>

<h3>It usually costs more</h3>
<p>Horizontal slat fences are generally more expensive than standard vertical panels. Because the boards are the focal point, many people choose a premium grade of timber with few knots, rather than the basic pressure-treated softwood used for everyday fencing. Labour is higher too: measuring, levelling and fixing each board takes much longer than putting up ready-made panels, and the extra posts add to the total. Get itemised quotes for the same material, height, post spacing and finish so you can compare them fairly.</p>

<h3>Horizontal surfaces hold water</h3>
<p>Upkeep depends on the material. Timber needs regular staining, oiling or sealing to slow greying and reduce warping from sun and moisture. Horizontal boards also hold water on their top edges for longer than vertical boards, which makes timber more prone to water damage if it is left untreated. A slight bevel on the top edge of each slat, a top cap or rail over the fence and gaps that let the boards dry out all help. Aluminium and good-quality composite are low maintenance by comparison, usually needing little more than an occasional wash; follow the manufacturer's cleaning guidance.</p>

<h3>The "ladder effect"</h3>
<p>Horizontal slats can act like the rungs of a ladder, giving children and agile pets a foothold, which makes them easier to climb than flat vertical boards. Very small gaps, a smooth face on the outward side or a taller design all reduce this. Around a swimming pool, check your local pool-barrier rules before choosing horizontal slats, as many codes restrict climbable fences in these areas.</p>

<!--figure:weathered-horizontal-fence-planks|Horizontal boards can cup, sag and weather if the timber or spacing is wrong.-->

<h2 id="materials">3. Materials for a horizontal slat fence</h2>
<p>The material defines both the look and how the fence ages:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hardwoods (ipe, mangaris, teak):</strong> dense, naturally resistant to decay and handsome, but usually the most expensive option. Buy only from FSC- or PEFC-certified sources, as some tropical hardwoods are not sustainably harvested.</li>
<li><strong>Softwoods (cedar, redwood, larch):</strong> naturally attractive and decay-resistant, and the most common choice. They need more maintenance than dense hardwoods.</li>
<li><strong>Composite:</strong> made from wood fibres and plastic, it gives a timber look with less upkeep. It is heavy, so it needs strong posts and the support spacing the manufacturer specifies.</li>
<li><strong>Aluminium or steel:</strong> ideal for a modern, industrial look. Metal slats will not warp or rot and feel very secure.</li>
</ul>

<!--figure:timber-slats-closeup|Even, well-chosen slats with consistent gaps are what make the look work.-->

<h2 id="who-is-it-right-for">4. Who is a horizontal slat fence right for?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The modernist:</strong> if your home has clean lines, large windows and a minimalist style, a slatted fence is a natural match.</li>
<li><strong>The small-garden owner:</strong> if you want your outdoor space to feel bigger and more open, the widening effect of horizontal lines is a real benefit.</li>
<li><strong>The privacy seeker:</strong> if you want a tall, solid screen that does not look like a blank wall, slats give a more refined way to get seclusion, within local height limits.</li>
<li><strong>Homeowners planning to sell a contemporary home:</strong> a well-built slatted fence can add appeal for buyers, though any effect on value depends on your local market and the fence's condition.</li>
</ul>
<p>It is less suited to those who want the cheapest possible boundary, a quick DIY panel job, or a fence around a pool or play area where climbing is a concern.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before ordering materials, fix two or three sample slats to a post at your planned gap and view them from your seating area and from inside the house. It is the easiest way to judge privacy, light and the look of the spacing.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does a horizontal fence cost more than a vertical one?</h3>
<p>Usually, yes. It tends to use higher-grade boards, more posts and more labour for precise levelling. How much more depends on the material, height and length, so compare itemised quotes for the same specification.</p>
<h3>Will the boards sag over time?</h3>
<p>Sagging can be avoided with closer post spacing, boards of a suitable thickness, well-dried timber or metal, and the support spacing your supplier recommends.</p>
<h3>Is it DIY-friendly?</h3>
<p>It is harder than a standard panel fence, because every board must be level and evenly spaced. Confident DIYers can manage it with care and good tools, but many people prefer a professional installer. Our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-wood-fence">how to build a wood fence</a> covers setting out and posts.</p>
<h3>How long does a horizontal fence last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the material, the installation, your climate and the maintenance. As a general comparison, aluminium, composite and dense hardwoods tend to outlast basic softwood. Check the manufacturer's or supplier's warranty for its stated term.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A horizontal slat fence is more than a barrier; it is a design statement. It brings clean lines, adjustable privacy and a garden that feels wider, in return for a more demanding build, closer posts and, for timber, regular care. Choose the right material, plan for good drainage on the boards and check planning and safety rules first, and it can be a striking, long-lasting boundary. For more ideas, see <a href="/blog/horizontal-slat-fence-small-yard-ideas">horizontal slat fence ideas for small yards</a> and <a href="/blog/wood-fence-pros-and-cons">wood fence pros and cons</a>, or read <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-wood-fence">how to build a wood fence</a>. For help designing your outdoor space, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
